%U A400139 4,8,2,8,2,4,4,4,4,8,2,6,3,4,2,8,4,4,4,8,2,8,4,4,4,4,4,12
%N A400139 The number of divisors d of n such that gcid(d, n/d) is a powerful number (A001694), where gcid is the greatest common infinitary divisor.
%C A400139 First differs from A376887 at n = 64.
%H A400139 Amiram Eldar, <a href="/A400139/b400139.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%F A400139 Multiplicative with a(p^e) = e + 1 if e is odd, and e + 1 - 2^A000120((e-2)/2) if e is even.
%t A400139 f[p_, e_] := e + 1 - If[OddQ[e], 0, 2^DigitSum[(e-2)/2, 2]];
%t A400139 a[1] = 1; a[n_] := Times @@ f @@@ FactorInteger[n]; Array[a, 100]
%o A400139 (PARI) f(e) = e + 1 - if(!(e % 2), 2^hammingweight((e-2)/2));
%o A400139 a(n) = vecprod(apply(f, factor(n)[, 2]));
%Y A400139 Cf. A000120, A001694, A369310 (analogous with gcd), A376887, A399829 (unitary analog), A400137.
%Y A400139 The number of divisors d of n such that gcid(d, n/d) is: A037445 (1), A400138 (squarefree), this sequence (powerful), A400140 (square), A400141 (exponentially odd), A400142 (odd), A400143 (power of 2).
%K A400139 nonn,mult,easy,new
%O A400139 1,2
